@page "/data/arrow"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Snapshot
@using ExGrid.Docs.Pages.Pivot

<NativeControls />

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid.Data.Arrow" Title="Apache Arrow"
          Lead="A Snapshot written as an Arrow IPC stream and read back: how a server sends one to a browser, and how what pyarrow, DuckDB or Polars produced is taken in as it is.">

    <DocsSection Title="Writing and reading">
        <p>
            <code>SnapshotArrow.WriteAsync(snapshot, stream)</code> writes an uncompressed Arrow IPC stream: the rows in the
            Snapshot's order, in record batches of 65,536 rows. Each kind gets its Arrow type, a Blank a null slot, and the
            captions, the Record Key and the version travel in the metadata, so a Snapshot round-trips whole. HTTP's own
            compression (gzip, Brotli) does the rest. On a server, the response body is the stream, with
            <code>SnapshotArrow.StreamMediaType</code> as its content type.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>SnapshotArrow.ReadAsync</code> reads a stream or an Arrow file (<code>.arrow</code>, Feather 2) into the
            Snapshot's own layout, record batch by record batch, in slices that yield between them, with progress and
            cancellation. In a browser, fetch the payload whole and read the bytes
            (<code>request.SetBrowserResponseStreamingEnabled(false)</code>, then <code>ReadAsByteArrayAsync</code>):
            streaming the response costs far more than the read. Uncompressed buffers are read where they lie, without a
            copy.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ArrowRoundTrip)" Files="@(["Examples/Snapshot/SnapshotGrid.cs", "Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            The stream never leaves the page here: it is written to memory and read straight back.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What crosses">
        <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Snapshot</th><th>Written as</th><th>Read from</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Text</td>
                    <td><code>dictionary&lt;int32, utf8&gt;</code>, the Snapshot's own dictionary</td>
                    <td><code>utf8</code>, <code>large_utf8</code>, <code>utf8_view</code>, or a dictionary of any of them</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Decimal</td>
                    <td><code>decimal128(38, scale)</code> at the largest scale its values hold</td>
                    <td><code>decimal32</code> to <code>decimal256</code>, within <code>decimal</code>'s range and 28 places</td>
                </tr>
                <tr><td>Double</td><td><code>float64</code></td><td><code>float64</code>, <code>float32</code>, as they came</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Integer</td><td><code>int64</code></td><td><code>int8</code> to <code>int64</code>, <code>uint8</code> to <code>uint32</code>, <code>uint64</code> up to <code>long.MaxValue</code></td></tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Date</td>
                    <td><code>date32</code> when every value is a midnight, otherwise a <code>timestamp</code> without a time zone</td>
                    <td><code>date32</code>, <code>date64</code>, <code>timestamp</code> without a time zone or in UTC, <code>time32</code>, <code>time64</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr><td>Boolean</td><td><code>bool</code></td><td><code>bool</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Blank</td><td>a null slot</td><td>a null slot, or a null dictionary entry or index</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </MudSimpleTable>
        <p>
            Another producer's dictionary is taken under the Snapshot's rules: one entry per exact text, so
            <code>amer</code> and <code>AMER</code> are two. The metadata keys are <code>exgrid.caption</code> on a field and
            <code>exgrid.recordKey</code> and <code>exgrid.version</code> on the schema (<code>SnapshotArrowMetadata</code>);
            a read honours them whoever wrote them.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What is refused">
        <p>
            Anything a Snapshot cannot hold exactly is refused by name, and the read yields nothing:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>A type outside the table names its column: lists, structs, maps, binary, durations, intervals, <code>float16</code>, and a <code>timestamp</code> in a time zone other than UTC, which only a time zone database could convert.</li>
            <li>A value names its row and its column: a decimal beyond <code>decimal</code>'s range, a date outside 0001 to 9999, a nanosecond finer than the 100 ns a date holds.</li>
            <li>A stream cut short is never read as fewer rows, and bytes that are not Arrow at all, or still compressed with gzip, are refused saying so.</li>
            <li>A stream whose buffers are compressed (LZ4 frame or ZSTD, as pyarrow can write) is read when you pass <code>Apache.Arrow.Compression</code>'s <code>CompressionCodecFactory</code> as <code>codecs</code>, and refused without it, naming the codec.</li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ArrowRefusals)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="API">
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(global::ExGrid.Data.Arrow.SnapshotArrow)" />
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
